**Design notes**

| Concern | How it is addressed |
|---|---|
| **Encryption at rest** | Every secret value is encrypted with **AES-256-GCM**. A random 12-byte IV is generated per `put`, so identical plaintexts produce identical-length but different ciphertexts. An eavesreader sees only `IV ‖ ciphertext+tag` (base64-encoded) and learns only the approximate plaintext length. |
| **Owner / name isolation** | Each secret lives under its own storage key `__ss__sec__<base64(length-prefixed owner‖name)>`. No cross-owner or cross-name lookup is possible. |
| **Tampering detection** | AES-GCM's authentication tag means any modification of the ciphertext (bit-flips, truncation, replacement with another secret's blob) causes `subtle.decrypt` to reject. Additionally, the **(owner, name) pair is used as GCM additional authenticated data (AAD)**, so even *copying* a valid ciphertext from one slot to another fails authentication. The length-prefixed encoding (`[4-byte BE owner-len][owner UTF-8][name UTF-8]`) makes the AAD unambiguous even when owner or name contain NUL bytes. |
| **Wrong master password** | PBKDF2 derives a different AES key, so every `get` on existing secrets throws. `createSecretStore` itself succeeds (it only reads/creates the salt), satisfying the "either reject or throw" requirement via the `get`-throws path. |
| **Persistence / statelessness** | All state (salt, encrypted blobs, per-owner index) lives in the caller-supplied `KeyValueStorage`. A fresh `createSecretStore` with the same password and storage re-derives the same key and can read everything. `remove` physically deletes the secret's storage entry. |
| **Index integrity** | The per-owner name index (`__ss__idx__…`) is plain JSON; it is metadata only and contains no secret values. A corrupted index degrades to `list → []` but does not leak or corrupt secret data. |

